# compress.py — the end-to-end rebuild, and its honest boundaries

> **GENERATION-7 REFRESH (2026-09-02).** The shipped archive is AFR1,
> `cbb8d928a8ccdd3f5103da1d4a8d38d0662a5e5615266b923b5f8350d405bf25` at
> 180,002 bytes. As of 2026-09-01 this entry point **expresses that archive
> end-to-end**: its `AFR1_CHAIN` registry runs the five post-rc2 lossless
> stages with the real landed tools, and two complete chain runs from the
> retained pinned base each reproduced these exact bytes
> (receipt `RESULT_pq2_e2e.json`; verification memo
> `ddm_ce1_afr1_compress_chain_20260901.md` in the source repository).
> Earlier packet generations shipped a `compress.py` that refused this SHA by
> name because it could not express those stages; that refusal-era text is
> superseded by the measurement above.

This is the compression-side entry point for the submission. It is included because the
challenge asks for one and because a submission you cannot inspect is a submission you
cannot trust.

## The honest headline

`compress.py` **rebuilds the shipped `archive.zip` byte-identically** from pinned,
sha-verified inputs. The AFR1 chain replays the five lossless post-processing stages that
produced this archive from its base body, retains every intermediate archive, and refuses
unless **two complete runs** both reproduce the pinned 180,002-byte archive exactly. There
is no "close enough": a mismatch exits non-zero.

The chain runs are deterministic by construction — the corrector is integer/rational
arithmetic and the range coder is exact — and determinism is checked by measurement, not
asserted: `build` writes a second archive from the same member and asserts the two are
byte-identical.

## What the chain replays, exactly

The registered `AFR1_CHAIN` (`afr1_from_retained_rc2`) starts from the retained base
archive `df7fd266e1b7488cdec02c7b5c1201c40628804260286001f38b51d7ed9e2080`
(180,456 bytes) and runs five lossless stages, each pinned to its expected output SHA-256:

| Stage | What it does | Pinned output (prefix) |
|---|---|---|
| fx5 | fixed-point integer log-odds context-mixing re-encode | `4b54fccc` |
| dx2 | group-conditioned token-context re-encode | `976f706d` |
| gb1 | address-free tile-conditioned re-encode (tile48 × groupbin8) | `ec0dd68f` |
| lb1 | banked-lossless joint collect (consumes the jt21 bank) | `5b856e66` |
| afr1 | address-free tile48 × groupbin8 final re-encode | `cbb8d928` |

Every stage is lossless: the decoded token field is unchanged, so the score delta across
the chain is pure rate. The chain adds roughly 15–50 minutes of CPU time (dominated by two
full 600-pair re-encodes); inflation is unchanged.

## What is documented rather than re-run

- **Stage A (training).** Training the checkpoint from raw video is days of GPU compute.
  `compress.py provenance` emits the lineage instead: the stage scripts, their arguments,
  the corrector module, and an input manifest with a sha256 for every input. Everything
  downstream of the pinned inputs — the part that determines the archive bytes — is what
  the chain re-runs and verifies.
- **The content-deciding solve stages.** The base body's chain re-decided content before
  the lossless stages ran: the segmentation token edit solve over 573 pairs
  (`ddm_jg3_joint_solve.py`), the jg4 splice, the joint edit-admission waterfill
  (`ddm_jg5_pose_resolve_on_edited_renders.py`, 455 of 573 admitted), and the pose-carrier
  re-solve (`ddm_up3_carrier_splice.py::build_archive`, solver
  `ddm_br1_pose_basis_reorientation.py::gn_solve_pair`). These ship as their own scripts
  with receipts; the chain consumes their pinned output as its base input rather than
  re-deciding content.
- **Candidates outside the grammar.** A candidate whose rebuild would require re-deciding
  content is named in `NOT_EXPRESSIBLE` and refused by name, with its real builders cited.
  That refusal path still exists — it just no longer applies to the shipped archive.

## Requirements

- **Python 3.11+** (uses `hashlib.file_digest`). No third-party packages in this file: its
  only imports outside the standard library are from the `tac` research package (below).
- **It runs from the research repository, not from this submission directory.** It
  resolves the repository layout for `tac` imports and shells out to the landed stage
  tools. Copied into a bare submission directory it will not import.
- Inputs are supplied by `--inputs-json` or environment variables; run
  `python3 compress.py --chain afr1 --emit-inputs-template` to print the manifest,
  including the expected sha256 of each pinned input (the retained rc2 base among them).
  There are no local-path defaults.
- Storage preflight: the chain refuses to start with less than 8 GiB free under `--store`.

## Usage

```bash
# Print the AFR1 chain's input manifest with every pinned sha256. Works anywhere.
python3 compress.py --chain afr1 --emit-inputs-template

# The full AFR1 rebuild: five stages, every intermediate retained,
# two complete runs required, final bytes asserted against the pinned SHA.
python3 compress.py --stage chain --chain afr1 --chain-repeats 2 \
    --store ./build --inputs-json inputs.json

# 'all' selects the registered chain automatically when the expected archive has one.
python3 compress.py --stage all --store ./build --inputs-json inputs.json \
    --expected-archive-sha256 cbb8d928a8ccdd3f5103da1d4a8d38d0662a5e5615266b923b5f8350d405bf25 \
    --expected-archive-bytes 180002
```

Stages: `provenance`, `encode`, `build`, `verify`, `decode`, `chain`, `all`. Which
candidate is expected is resolved at run time (explicit flags, a sealed candidate runtime
via `--candidate-runtime`, or the current frontier pointer) — never latched in the file.
`--resume` continues bit-faithfully from a retained frame-boundary checkpoint.

`decode` (stage C) proves the rebuilt archive's decoded token field reproduces its pinned
sha256 through our internal receiver harness. Honest limitation: that harness resolves one
source root from local custody, so it is a repository proof rather than a judge-runnable
one. A judge does not need it — the shipped `inflate.sh` is the real decode path and is
what the evaluator runs.

## Axis

`[macOS-CPU advisory / scorer-free EXACT byte measurement]`. This script measures bytes
and byte-identity only. It makes no score claim: the score on the rebuilt bytes is
whatever the contest evaluator returns on the contest hardware.

## Known gaps

1. **The expressibility registry is hand-maintained.** A future candidate outside the
   grammar that nobody has registered falls through to a less precise message.
2. **Stage C is repository-scoped** (above): the byte-identity decode proof needs local
   custody; the judge-facing decode path is `inflate.sh`.
3. **Test coverage is by execution.** The chain, refusal, and happy paths were verified by
   running them (two complete chain runs, receipt retained), not by a unit-test suite.
